blished there is no evidence to move the estimate away from the state's fundamentals.", "watch": "A sharp national shift toward Republicans or a divisive Democratic nominee could narrow the margin, while a favorable national environment would push the race toward Likely D.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No expert ratings published yet; forecast rests on fundamentals and polls.", "New or vacant seat with no expert ratings; fundamentals only.", "No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings."]}, "overview": "New York remains one of the most reliably Democratic states in presidential voting, with a partisan lean of D+8 and a long record of backing the Democrat at the top of the ticket. No incumbent is on the ballot in 2028, since the sitting president is term-limited, which removes the usual incumbency effects in both directions.\n\nRace-specific evidence is still thin. No polls of New York, expert ratings, or notable fundraising figures are available, so the outlook rests on fundamentals. Headlines so far concern the Democratic primary, with Ocasio-Cortez, Newsom, Harris and Ossoff discussed as contenders, and one national poll shows a Democrat within striking distance of Vance. Until state polling arrives, the outlook stays anchored to New York's Democratic lean, with uncertainty kept wide.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.8251, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-24", "p_dem": 0.7779, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-25", "p_dem": 0.769, "margin": 8.0}, {"date": "2026-09-26", "p_dem": 0.7679, "margin": 8.0}]}